; ARBITRARY PRECISION ON JAVASCRIPT, WHICH IS `BigInt` IN THE LANGUAGE ITSELF.
;
; ADR 0019's third escape lands here: a range declared above the portable window
; promotes that value to arbitrary precision, and on this host the host already
; has it. See targets/go/bigint.oro for the shared argument.
;
; THIS IS THE HOST WHERE THE OPERATORS SURVIVE. `+`, `-`, `*`, `<` all work on
; `BigInt` directly, so the templates are the ordinary ones -- but they are a
; SEPARATE declaration rather than a reuse of `js.+`, because mixing a Number
; and a BigInt with `+` is a TypeError at run time. The compiler is what keeps
; them apart, and it can only do that if the two are two names.
;
; `/` TRUNCATES TOWARD ZERO on BigInt, which is not what `/` does on Number --
; there it is float division, and targets/js/builtin.oro has to declare
; `Math.trunc(a / b)` to get the language's meaning. So the divergence
; integers.md found at the word level does not exist at this one: BigInt is the
; better-behaved of V8's two integer stories.
;
; AND NEGATIVE ZERO DOES NOT EXIST HERE, which is the other word-level hazard
; gone: `-0n` is `0n`, so the `+ 0` normalisation `js./` and `js.%` need has
; nothing to do. Two hazards this file does not inherit, both worth stating
; because both cost a differential case at the word level.
;
; A KNOWN COST: bigarith-2026-08-28 measured V8's `BigInt` losing to a bitwise
; limb form up to about 100 limbs and winning above it, because `BigInt` is C++
; with real 64-bit limbs and JS has no 64x64->128 multiply at all. So this is
; the RIGHT choice for large values and the wrong one for the small ones
; precision integers actually reach -- and that is a THRESHOLD, which is a thing
; a target declares. Not declared yet: the fixed-limb rung is not built.

(target js
  ; ═══ HOW THIS TARGET STORES A BOUNDED BIG VALUE
  ;
  ; A range is SEMANTICS -- `(int 0 (pow 2 1300))` says the value is a
  ; mathematical integer in that interval, which is true on every target and is
  ; what a programmer is taught. How it is STORED is this file's business,
  ; exactly as `int-repr` decides one rung down that 0..255 is a `[]byte` on Go
  ; and a `short[]` on the JVM.
  ;
  ; `BigInt`, and this is where the SHAPE of a declaration was costing the most:
  ; 200! is 530,870 ns in limbs against 6,886 through `BigInt`, a factor of 77
  ; for the same computation. `BigInt` is C++ with real 64-bit limbs, and V8 has
  ; no 64x64 multiply to offer a library written in this language -- ours are
  ; 24-bit with `/` for the carry, because ADR 0012 forces 2W < 53 and bitwise
  ; operators are Tier 2. This host is unlikely ever to prefer ours.
  (big-repr host)

  ; JavaScript has one number type and this is the second one. It is written for
  ; the checker only; the emitter writes no types on this host.
  (type big "bigint")

  (module js
    (prim big+ (big big) big expr "(%s + %s)" pure)
    (prim big- (big big) big expr "(%s - %s)" pure)
    (prim big* (big big) big expr "(%s * %s)" pure)
    (prim big/ ((a big) (b big)) big expr "(%s / %s)" pure)
    (prim big% ((a big) (b big)) big expr "(%s %% %s)" pure)

    (prim big<  (big big) bool expr "(%s < %s)"   pure)
    (prim big<= (big big) bool expr "(%s <= %s)"  pure)
    (prim big>  (big big) bool expr "(%s > %s)"   pure)
    (prim big>= (big big) bool expr "(%s >= %s)"  pure)
    ; `===` and not `==`: `1n == 1` is TRUE and `1n === 1` is false. Equality
    ; across the two representations is exactly the confusion this file exists
    ; to prevent, so the strict form is the only correct one.
    (prim big=  (big big) bool expr "(%s === %s)" pure)

    (prim big-of (int) big expr "BigInt(%s)" pure)
    ; NOT `%s.toString()`: `String(x)` gives "123" where a template literal or a
    ; bare console.log of a BigInt gives "123n". The suffix is a real)
    ; divergence from the other two hosts and this is where it is removed.
    (prim big-str (big) string expr "String(%s)" pure)
    ; THE DECLARED BOUND, ENFORCED -- see targets/go/bigint.oro for why the two
    ; representations must agree about it.
    ;
    ; A SHIFT rather than a comparison against 2^k, because building that value
    ; would allocate a `BigInt` per call. `BigInt(oroK)` still converts a small
    ; number per call, and emitting the count as a `BigInt` LITERAL instead is
    ; measured at 1.14x here (8,427 ns against 9,565 on 200!) -- available, and
    ; not taken, because it would mean putting the bound FIRST in the
    ; primitive's arguments on all three hosts to get it into the template body.
    ; The check itself costs 1.32x over no check on this host.
    (prim big-fit ((x big) (k int)) big expr "(function(oroZ, oroK){ if ((oroZ >> BigInt(oroK)) !== 0n) throw new Error(\"bignum overflow: the declared range is too small\"); return oroZ; })(%s, %s)")
    ; The fixed-limb rung's carry check -- see targets/go/bigint.oro for why a
    ; declared width has to fail loudly rather than truncate.
    (prim trap-if (int) int expr "(function(oroC){ if (oroC !== 0) throw new Error(\"bignum overflow: the declared range is too small\"); return 0; })(%s)")))
